## Write your first configuration

Create `main.tf`:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws", version = "~> 5.0" }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "notes" {
  bucket = "my-unique-notes-bucket-12345"
}
```

## Run the core workflow

Then run the core workflow:

```bash
terraform init     # download providers
terraform plan     # preview
terraform apply    # create
terraform destroy  # clean up
```

**Practice:** Create and destroy a bucket, then read the generated `terraform.tfstate`.

## Inputs, outputs and reuse

Hard-coded values do not scale. Use inputs and outputs:

```hcl
variable "env" {
  type    = string
  default = "dev"
}

locals {
  name = "app-${var.env}"
}

output "bucket_arn" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.notes.arn
}
```

Set values with `-var env=prod`, a `terraform.tfvars` file, or `TF_VAR_env` environment variables.

## Create many similar resources

Use `count` and `for_each` to create many similar resources:

```hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "logs" {
  for_each = toset(["web", "api"])
  bucket   = "${local.name}-${each.key}-logs"
}
```

## Records and sealed types

```java
public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square {}
public record Circle(double r) implements Shape {}
public record Square(double side) implements Shape {}

static double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c -> Math.PI * c.r() * c.r();
        case Square q -> q.side() * q.side();
    };
}
```

## Why these types help

Records are immutable data carriers; sealed types plus pattern matching give exhaustive switches.

**Prerequisites:** "Collections and streams", lambdas and exceptions.

## What you will learn
You will run tasks through an executor and learn when Java 21 virtual threads help.

## Threads and executors
Threads in one JVM share the heap, so two threads can change the same object. That sharing brings both the speed-up and the bugs.

An `ExecutorService` separates what to run from how it runs, so you do not track threads by hand. You `submit` a task and get a `Future`. Its `get()` waits for the result, or throws an `ExecutionException` that wraps the task's exception. In Java 21, `ExecutorService` is `AutoCloseable`: try-with-resources shuts it down and waits for submitted tasks.

## Platform threads and virtual threads
A platform thread wraps an operating-system thread and costs real memory, so servers pool a few hundred. When every pooled thread is blocked on a database call, new requests queue while the CPU sits idle.

A virtual thread is a `Thread` that the JVM schedules on a few carrier platform threads. When it blocks on `sleep` or supported I/O, the JVM unmounts it and the carrier runs another. Virtual threads are final in Java 21. They help when tasks mostly wait, not with CPU-heavy work. Create one per task; do not pool them.

They change the cost of a thread, not the rules of correctness. Tasks that update the same `HashMap` still need a lock or a concurrent collection. Prefer tasks that return values, combined by one thread.

## Worked example
Save as `VirtualDemo.java` and run `java VirtualDemo.java`:

```java
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class VirtualDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        var futures = new ArrayList<Future<Integer>>();
        try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
                final int id = i;
                futures.add(executor.submit(() -> {
                    Thread.sleep(Duration.ofMillis(100)); // stands in for a network call
                    return id;
                }));
            }
        } // close() waits for every submitted task
        long sum = 0;
        for (var f : futures) sum += f.get();
        long ms = Duration.ofNanos(System.nanoTime() - start).toMillis();
        System.out.println("tasks=" + futures.size() + " sum=" + sum);
        System.out.println("elapsed under 5s: " + (ms < 5_000));
    }
}
```

Expected output:

```text
tasks=10000 sum=49995000
elapsed under 5s: true
```

Each task waits 100 ms. A pool of 100 platform threads would need about 10 seconds for 10,000 tasks. Virtual threads let them all wait at once.

## Practice and self-review
- Use `Executors.newFixedThreadPool(100)` instead. Why does the second line become `false`?
- Throw an exception when `id == 42`. Where does it surface, and as which type?
- Replace the sleep with 100 ms of computation. Do virtual threads still help? Then read "Threads, lifecycle and the Java Memory Model".

## References
- [Virtual threads](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/core/virtual-threads.html)
- [java.util.concurrent API](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/package-summary.html)

**Prerequisites:** JavaScript functions, and the previous lesson, *The event loop*.

## What you will learn
How promises represent work that finishes later, and how to use `async`/`await` to run work in sequence or in parallel without losing errors.

## Promises and await
A promise is a result that is not ready yet. It starts **pending** and settles once: **fulfilled** with a value or **rejected** with an error. An `async` function always returns a promise. Inside it, `await` pauses that function until the promise settles, then returns the value or throws the error. It does not block the thread: while one function waits, the event loop serves other work.

## Sequential or parallel
Each `await` waits for the one before it. That is right when step two needs step one's result. For independent calls, start them together and wait once. The total time is then the slowest call, not the sum.

- `Promise.all` rejects as soon as one promise rejects. Use it when you need every result.
- `Promise.allSettled` waits for all and reports each outcome. Use it when partial success is fine.

For long lists, work in batches so you do not overload a database or an API.

## Handle every rejection
Use `try`/`catch` where you can respond usefully. A rejection nobody handles is an **unhandled rejection**, and current Node versions exit with a non-zero code when one happens. Common causes are a missing `await` and no `.catch` on the top-level call.

## Worked example
Save as `async-demo.mjs` and run `node async-demo.mjs`. `sleep` stands in for a database call.

```javascript
import { setTimeout as sleep } from 'node:timers/promises';

async function fetchUser(id) {
  await sleep(100); // stands in for a database or HTTP call
  if (id < 0) throw new Error(`bad id ${id}`);
  return { id, name: `user-${id}` };
}

const elapsed = (start) => `${Math.round((Date.now() - start) / 100) * 100}ms`;

async function main() {
  let start = Date.now();
  const a = await fetchUser(1);
  const b = await fetchUser(2);
  console.log('sequential', a.name, b.name, elapsed(start));

  start = Date.now();
  const [c, d] = await Promise.all([fetchUser(3), fetchUser(4)]);
  console.log('parallel', c.name, d.name, elapsed(start));

  try {
    await fetchUser(-1);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log('caught', err.message);
  }

  const results = await Promise.allSettled([fetchUser(5), fetchUser(-2)]);
  console.log('settled', results.map((r) => r.status).join(','));
}

main().catch((err) => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

Expected output:

```text
sequential user-1 user-2 200ms
parallel user-3 user-4 100ms
caught bad id -1
settled fulfilled,rejected
```

The second sequential call starts only after the first finishes, so that pair takes twice as long.

## Practice and self-review
1. Remove the `try`/`catch` around `fetchUser(-1)`. What is printed, and what is the exit code?
2. Change `Promise.allSettled` to `Promise.all`. What happens, and why?
3. Write `fetchMany(ids, batchSize)` that fetches in batches with `Promise.all`.

## References
- [Node.js timers promises API](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest/api/timers.html)

**Prerequisites:** *Promises and async/await*, and HTTP basics: methods, status codes, headers.

## What you will learn
How a Node.js server handles requests, by building a small JSON API with the built-in `node:http` module.

## One handler, every request
`createServer` runs one function for every request. `req` is a readable stream with the method, URL, headers and body; `res` is where you write the status, headers and body. Nothing is routed or parsed for you, and every response must be ended or the client hangs. Collect the body yourself and cap its size, or one client can exhaust your memory.

## Status codes are your contract
Clients act on status codes: `201` created, `400` invalid input, `404` missing, `413` too large, and `500` for bugs, without a stack trace. Catch async errors in one place, because a rejected promise does not reach the server by itself.

## Where frameworks fit
Express, Fastify and NestJS add routing, parsing and middleware on this same model. For production, add timeouts and graceful shutdown (see *npm and production readiness*).

## Worked example
Save as `server.mjs` and run `node server.mjs`. It needs no packages.

```javascript
import { createServer } from 'node:http';

const notes = new Map();
let nextId = 1;

const httpError = (status, message) => Object.assign(new Error(message), { status });

function send(res, status, body) {
  res.writeHead(status, { 'content-type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify(body));
}

async function readJson(req) {
  let raw = '';
  req.setEncoding('utf8');
  for await (const chunk of req) {
    raw += chunk;
    if (raw.length > 10_000) throw httpError(413, 'body too large');
  }
  try {
    return JSON.parse(raw);
  } catch {
    throw httpError(400, 'invalid JSON');
  }
}

async function handle(req, res) {
  const { pathname } = new URL(req.url, 'http://localhost');
  if (req.method === 'POST' && pathname === '/notes') {
    const body = await readJson(req);
    if (typeof body?.text !== 'string') throw httpError(400, 'text is required');
    const note = { id: nextId++, text: body.text };
    notes.set(note.id, note);
    return send(res, 201, note);
  }
  const id = Number(pathname.match(/^\/notes\/(\d+)$/)?.[1]);
  if (req.method === 'GET' && notes.has(id)) return send(res, 200, notes.get(id));
  throw httpError(404, 'not found');
}

createServer((req, res) => {
  handle(req, res).catch((err) => {
    if (!err.status) console.error(err);
    send(res, err.status ?? 500, { error: err.status ? err.message : 'internal error' });
  });
}).listen(3000);
```

In a second terminal:

```bash
curl -X POST localhost:3000/notes -d '{"text":"learn node:http"}'
curl localhost:3000/notes/1
curl localhost:3000/notes/7
curl -X POST localhost:3000/notes -d '{}'
```

Expected output:

```text
{"id":1,"text":"learn node:http"}
{"id":1,"text":"learn node:http"}
{"error":"not found"}
{"error":"text is required"}
```

Add `-i` to see status lines such as `HTTP/1.1 201 Created`.

## Practice and self-review
1. Send a body over 10,000 characters. Which status comes back?
2. Add `DELETE /notes/:id` returning `204` or `404`.
3. Throw an error inside `handle`. Confirm the client sees only `internal error`.

## References
- [Node.js HTTP module](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest/api/http.html)

**Prerequisites:** "Go basics and modules": modules, slices and maps.

## What you will learn
You will define structs with methods, satisfy interfaces implicitly, and return, wrap and inspect errors.

## Structs and methods
A struct groups named fields. Go has no classes and no inheritance; you attach behaviour with methods. A pointer receiver, as in `func (m *memStore) Put(...)`, can change the struct and avoids a copy. If one method needs a pointer receiver, give all the type's methods one. Reuse behaviour by composing small types.

## Interfaces are satisfied implicitly
An interface lists method signatures. Any type with those methods satisfies it; there is no `implements` keyword. So the code that uses a dependency can declare the small interface it needs, and tests can pass in a fake. The line `var _ Store = (*memStore)(nil)` makes the compiler confirm that `*memStore` satisfies `Store`.

## Errors are values
A function that can fail returns an `error` as its last result, and the caller checks it with `if err != nil`. A sentinel such as `var ErrNotFound = errors.New("not found")` names a failure callers may detect.

When you pass an error up, add context with `fmt.Errorf("load %s: %w", id, err)`. The `%w` verb wraps the original, so it stays reachable. `errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound)` looks through every layer of wrapping, while `err == ErrNotFound` fails once the error is wrapped. `errors.As` extracts an error of a specific type. Keep `panic` for programmer bugs, not for missing records or bad input.

## Worked example
In a new module, save as `main.go`:

```go
package main

import (
	"errors"
	"fmt"
)

type Store interface {
	Get(id string) (string, error)
}

var ErrNotFound = errors.New("not found")

type memStore struct {
	items map[string]string
}

func (m *memStore) Get(id string) (string, error) {
	v, ok := m.items[id]
	if !ok {
		return "", ErrNotFound
	}
	return v, nil
}

var _ Store = (*memStore)(nil) // compile-time check

func (m *memStore) Put(id, v string) { m.items[id] = v }

func load(s Store, id string) (string, error) {
	v, err := s.Get(id)
	if err != nil {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("load %s: %w", id, err)
	}
	return v, nil
}

func main() {
	store := &memStore{items: map[string]string{}}
	store.Put("go-101", "Go basics")

	for _, id := range []string{"go-101", "rust-101"} {
		v, err := load(store, id)
		switch {
		case errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound):
			fmt.Println("missing:", err)
		case err != nil:
			fmt.Println("failed:", err)
		default:
			fmt.Println("found:", v)
		}
	}
}
```

Run `go run .`. Expected output:

```text
found: Go basics
missing: load rust-101: not found
```

## Practice and self-review
- Change `%w` to `%v` in `load`. Which `switch` branch runs now, and why?
- Write a second `Store` backed by a slice and pass it to `load` unchanged.
- Define a `NotFoundError` struct type, return it from `Get`, and detect it with `errors.As`.
- Delete `Get` and read the compiler error.

## References
- [Effective Go](https://go.dev/doc/effective_go)
- [Working with errors](https://go.dev/blog/go1.13-errors)

**Prerequisites:** Java basics (classes, records, annotations) and JDK 17 or later.

## What you will learn
How to generate a Spring Boot project, what auto-configuration does with your dependencies, and how `application.yml` and profiles change behaviour without code changes.

## Generate the project
At start.spring.io, choose Maven, Java and the current Spring Boot version, and add only **Spring Web**. You get a `pom.xml` that inherits managed library versions from `spring-boot-starter-parent`, the `mvnw` Maven wrapper so every machine builds the same way, and a `DemoApplication` class with a `main` method.

## Auto-configuration
A **starter** is one dependency that pulls in a tested set of libraries; the web starter brings Spring MVC, Jackson and embedded Tomcat. `@SpringBootApplication` turns on auto-configuration: at startup Spring Boot inspects the classpath and your properties and creates the beans you would otherwise write, here a server on port 8080 with JSON support.

Each auto-configuration is conditional and backs off when you define your own bean. This also explains a common first failure: add **Spring Data JPA** with no `spring.datasource.url` and no embedded database, and startup fails because there is nothing to connect to. Start with `--debug` to see which conditions matched.

## Configuration and profiles
Properties come from `application.yml`, environment variables and command-line arguments, in increasing priority. A **profile** such as `dev` loads `application-dev.yml` on top. Keep secrets out of these files and pass them as environment variables.

## Worked example
Replace `DemoApplication.java` with:

```java
package com.example.demo;

import java.util.Map;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}

@RestController
class GreetingController {
    private final String greeting;

    GreetingController(@Value("${app.greeting}") String greeting) {
        this.greeting = greeting;
    }

    @GetMapping("/hello")
    Map<String, String> hello() {
        return Map.of("message", greeting);
    }
}
```

`src/main/resources/application.yml`:

```yaml
app:
  greeting: Hello from Spring Boot
```

`src/main/resources/application-dev.yml`:

```yaml
app:
  greeting: Hello from the dev profile
```

Run the app, then call it from a second terminal:

```bash
./mvnw spring-boot:run
curl localhost:8080/hello
```

Expected output:

```text
{"message":"Hello from Spring Boot"}
```

Restart with `./mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.profiles=dev` and the same request returns `{"message":"Hello from the dev profile"}`.

## Practice and self-review
1. Start with the environment variable `APP_GREETING=Hi`. Which value wins, and why?
2. Set `server.port: 9090` and confirm it in the startup log.
3. Add Spring Data JPA without a database. Explain the error in one sentence.

## References
- [Spring Boot documentation](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/)
- [Externalized configuration](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/features/external-config.html)

**Prerequisites:** *Bootstrap a project*, with the **Validation** dependency added.

## What you will learn
How to map HTTP requests to Java methods, return correct status codes, and reject invalid input before it reaches business logic.

## Map requests to methods
`@RestController` writes return values to the response body as JSON. `@RequestMapping("/books")` sets a base path; `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping` and `@DeleteMapping` map methods. `@PathVariable` reads part of the path, `@RequestParam` reads a query parameter, and `@RequestBody` converts JSON into an object, often a record.

Return `ResponseEntity` when status or headers vary. `ResponseEntity.created(uri)` gives `201` with a `Location` header; `ResponseEntity.of(optional)` gives `200`, or `404` when empty.

## Validate at the boundary
Annotate the request record with constraints such as `@NotBlank` or `@Size`, and the parameter with `@Valid`. Spring checks them before your method runs and answers `400 Bad Request` on failure. Shaping that error body is covered in *Global error handling and API design*. Use a separate request type, not your entity, so clients cannot set fields such as `id`.

## Keep controllers thin
A controller translates HTTP into a method call. Business rules belong in a `@Service`, which tests and message consumers can reuse without HTTP.

## Worked example
Save as `Books.java` next to `DemoApplication.java`:

```java
package com.example.demo;

import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import java.net.URI;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

record Book(long id, String title, String author) {}

record BookRequest(@NotBlank String title, @NotBlank String author) {}

@Service
class BookService {
    private final Map<Long, Book> books = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final AtomicLong ids = new AtomicLong();

    Book create(BookRequest request) {
        Book book = new Book(ids.incrementAndGet(), request.title(), request.author());
        books.put(book.id(), book);
        return book;
    }

    Optional<Book> find(long id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(books.get(id));
    }
}

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/books")
class BookController {
    private final BookService service;

    BookController(BookService service) {
        this.service = service;
    }

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<Book> create(@Valid @RequestBody BookRequest request) {
        Book book = service.create(request);
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/books/" + book.id())).body(book);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    ResponseEntity<Book> get(@PathVariable long id) {
        return ResponseEntity.of(service.find(id));
    }
}
```

Run `./mvnw spring-boot:run`, then:

```bash
curl -i localhost:8080/books -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"title":"Effective Java","author":"Joshua Bloch"}'
curl -i localhost:8080/books/99
curl -i localhost:8080/books -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"title":"","author":"Joshua Bloch"}'
```

Expected output, trimmed to the status lines and the created book:

```text
HTTP/1.1 201
Location: /books/1
{"id":1,"title":"Effective Java","author":"Joshua Bloch"}
HTTP/1.1 404
HTTP/1.1 400
```

## Practice and self-review
1. Add `GET /books?author=...` with `@RequestParam`.
2. Add `@Size(max = 200)` to `title` and test a longer title.
3. Add `DELETE /books/{id}` returning `204` or `404`.

## References
- [Spring Framework: annotated controllers](https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc/mvc-controller.html)

**Prerequisites:** *REST controllers and validation* and *Relational modelling* (Databases & SQL).

## What you will learn
How Spring Data JPA turns a repository interface into queries, and why Flyway migrations, not Hibernate, should own your schema.

## Entities and repositories
An `@Entity` class maps to a table; `@Id` marks the primary key, and `IDENTITY` lets the database assign it. Extend `JpaRepository<Book, Long>` and Spring implements `save`, `findById`, `count` and paging. It also derives queries from method names: `findByTitleContainingIgnoreCaseOrderByTitle` becomes a case-insensitive `LIKE` with `ORDER BY`.

## Migrations own the schema
`ddl-auto: update` alters tables from your entities, but cannot rename columns and keeps no history. Use **Flyway**: numbered SQL files in `db/migration`, applied in order and recorded in `flyway_schema_history`. Never edit a migration that has run; add a new one. Set `ddl-auto: validate` so startup fails if entities and tables disagree. In Spring Boot 4, add `spring-boot-starter-flyway`, plus `flyway-database-postgresql` for PostgreSQL.

## Transactions and query counts
Mark multi-step service methods `@Transactional` so they commit or roll back together; use `readOnly = true` for reads. Watch the SQL log for the **N+1 problem**: one query for a list, then one per row for a relationship (see *Hibernate relationships and query behavior*).

## Worked example
Generate a project with **Spring Data JPA**, **Flyway Migration** and **H2 Database** (in-memory, so no server).

`src/main/resources/application.yml`:

```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: validate
```

`src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__create_book.sql`:

```sql
CREATE TABLE book (
  id     BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  title  VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
  author VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL
);

INSERT INTO book (title, author) VALUES
  ('Effective Java', 'Joshua Bloch'),
  ('Java Concurrency in Practice', 'Brian Goetz');
```

`BooksApplication.java`, holding the entity, repository and a startup runner:

```java
package com.example.books;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

@Entity
class Book {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String title;
    private String author;

    protected Book() {} // required by JPA

    Book(String title, String author) {
        this.title = title;
        this.author = author;
    }

    String getTitle() { return title; }
}

interface BookRepository extends JpaRepository<Book, Long> {
    List<Book> findByTitleContainingIgnoreCaseOrderByTitle(String text);
}

@SpringBootApplication
public class BooksApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(BooksApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    CommandLineRunner demo(BookRepository books) {
        return args -> {
            books.save(new Book("Modern Java in Action", "Raoul-Gabriel Urma"));
            books.findByTitleContainingIgnoreCaseOrderByTitle("java")
                .forEach(b -> System.out.println(b.getTitle()));
            System.out.println("total=" + books.count());
        };
    }
}
```

Run `./mvnw spring-boot:run`. Expected output, after the logs:

```text
Effective Java
Java Concurrency in Practice
Modern Java in Action
total=3
```

## Practice and self-review
1. Add a `published` field to `Book` with no migration. What does `validate` report?
2. Fix it with `V2__add_published.sql`, then explain why `update` would be riskier.

## References
- [Spring Data JPA reference](https://docs.spring.io/spring-data/jpa/reference/)

**Prerequisites:** Modern JavaScript (`const`, arrow functions, objects, arrays) and Node.js installed.

## What you will learn
How TypeScript checks code before it runs, and the types you will use daily in NestJS: object shapes, unions, narrowing, generics and utility types.

## Checked, then erased
The compiler, `tsc`, checks your types and outputs plain JavaScript. Types do not exist at runtime, so data from an HTTP request still needs runtime validation (see *DTO validation and persistence*). Enable `"strict": true` in every project. It includes `strictNullChecks`, which makes `undefined` a separate type and catches many real bugs.

## Shapes, unions and narrowing
- An `interface` or `type` describes an object. `?` marks an optional property, whose type includes `undefined`.
- A **union** such as `'STARTER' | 'ADVANCED'` lists allowed values, so a typo becomes a compile error.
- **Narrowing**: after `typeof input === 'number'`, the compiler knows `input` is a number. `typeof`, truthiness checks and `in` all narrow unions.

## Generics and utility types
`first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined` works for any array and keeps the element type. `Partial<T>` makes every property optional, `Pick<T, K>` keeps some, and `Record<K, V>` maps keys to values. Build update payloads from them instead of copying interfaces.

## Worked example
Save as `essentials.ts`:

```typescript
type Level = 'STARTER' | 'INTERMEDIATE' | 'ADVANCED';

interface Lesson {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  level: Level;
  tags?: string[];
}

function parseId(input: string | number): number {
  if (typeof input === 'number') return input; // narrowed to number
  const id = Number.parseInt(input, 10); // narrowed to string
  if (Number.isNaN(id)) throw new Error(`not an id: ${input}`);
  return id;
}

function first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}

function describe(lesson: Lesson): string {
  const tags = lesson.tags ? lesson.tags.join(', ') : 'no tags';
  return `${lesson.id}: ${lesson.title} [${lesson.level}] (${tags})`;
}

const lessons: Lesson[] = [
  { id: parseId('1'), title: 'Types', level: 'STARTER', tags: ['basics'] },
  { id: parseId(2), title: 'Narrowing', level: 'STARTER' },
];
const patch: Partial<Pick<Lesson, 'title' | 'tags'>> = { title: 'Generics' };

const lesson = first(lessons); // Lesson | undefined
if (lesson) console.log(describe({ ...lesson, ...patch }));
console.log(describe(lessons[1]));
```

