## What you will learn
How a NestJS application is built from modules, controllers and providers, and how dependency injection wires them together.

## Three building blocks
- A **controller** handles HTTP. `@Controller('lessons')` with `@Get(':id')` handles `GET /lessons/:id`. It reads the request, calls a service and returns the result, which Nest sends as JSON.
- A **provider** is a class Nest creates and injects, usually an `@Injectable()` service. Business rules live here, testable without HTTP.
- A **module** groups one feature's controllers and providers. The root module imports feature modules, and Nest builds the app from that tree.

## Dependency injection
You never call `new LessonsService()`. You declare it as a constructor parameter, and Nest creates the instance and passes it in, matching on the parameter's type. Providers are singletons by default, so never keep per-request data in their fields.

Injection respects module boundaries. Another module can use a provider only if its module lists it in `exports` and the other module `imports` that module. When a dependency is not visible, startup fails with "Nest can't resolve dependencies", so wiring mistakes never reach production. Constructor injection also lets tests swap in fakes (see *Auth, guards and testing*).

## Pipes at the edge
`ParseIntPipe` turns the `id` route parameter into a number. A non-numeric value gets `400 Bad Request` before your method runs.

## Worked example
Run `npx @nestjs/cli new lessons-demo`, save this as `src/lessons.module.ts`, and add `LessonsModule` to the `imports` array in `src/app.module.ts`. The CLI usually puts each class in its own file.

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, Injectable, Module, NotFoundException, Param, ParseIntPipe } from '@nestjs/common';

export interface Lesson {
  id: number;
  title: string;
}

@Injectable()
export class LessonsService {
  private readonly lessons: Lesson[] = [
    { id: 1, title: 'TypeScript essentials' },
    { id: 2, title: 'NestJS modules' },
  ];

  find(id: number): Lesson {
    const lesson = this.lessons.find((l) => l.id === id);
    if (!lesson) throw new NotFoundException(`Lesson ${id} not found`);
    return lesson;
  }
}

@Controller('lessons')
export class LessonsController {
  constructor(private readonly service: LessonsService) {}

  @Get(':id')
  find(@Param('id', ParseIntPipe) id: number): Lesson {
    return this.service.find(id);
  }
}

@Module({
  controllers: [LessonsController],
  providers: [LessonsService],
  exports: [LessonsService],
})
export class LessonsModule {}
```

